ols but I know a lot of people who use  them and they find them really good  [Music]  there are a few different categories of  chemical exfoliants that you can find in  skincare  the most common chemical exfoliants are  the hydroxy acids in general these work  by speeding up how quickly skin cells  renew themselves and loosening the  layers of dead skin cells so that they  come off more easily in smaller chunks  they've also been found to even out skin  tone and lessen wrinkles the most common  ones are the alpha hydroxy acids like  glycolic acid lactic acid and mandelic  acid these are usually recommended if  you've got dry skin but I have oily skin  and I found them really really useful  for smoothing out my skin and fading  hyperpigmentation  alpha hydroxy acids work most  effectively at a lower pH so usually  products with a pH below  four and a concentration of between four  and ten percent are recommended when  you're just starting out with these  antes for air chase I really like lactic  acid there's a whole bunch of lattic  acid products that you can get as well  which are really good so for example  there's one from the ordinary there's  one that I really like from ultra suta  khals I also like the weaker tone  estelle hydroxy acid exfoliants like  Pixy glow tonic and crave Beauty  callaloo yeah because you can use them  more regularly without worrying about  irritation and you can use them I guess  you can incorporate them into your love  routine a bit more easily as well so you  don't have to worry about combining it  with other irritating things because  overall the irritation ends up being a  bit less if you're just starting with  exfoliation I wouldn't recommend one of  the stronger wash off AJ products yet  but if you're a bit further along and  you've got quite a lot in your routine I  like having these wash off products  because then you can just insert it at  some point during the week and you have  more space to move your other products  around so the ones that I've tried so  far kate Somerville's exfoli kate and  drunk elephants baby facial and both of  these seem to work pretty well I tend to  use them just before a shower I use them  like a mask and then I wash it off in  the shower salicylic acid is often  called beta hydroxy acid and because  it's more oil soluble than alpha hydroxy  acids it's often recommended for people  who have oily skin and acne there's been  a study that found that pH matters a lot  less for salicylic acid and it can  exfoliate effectively even around pH 7  which is neutral I have a video which  was actually my first video that talks  more about pH and acids that I've linked  in the caption if you're interested in  the finer details of how all of this  works in terms of beta hydroxy acid  exfoliants I always recommend Paula's  Choice 2% BHA liquid it's got a really  liquid texture it goes on really nicely  it shrinks blackheads and it's really  good at calming down pimples as well and  it lasts forever a newer type of hydroxy  acid is the poly hydroxy acid or PHA  this includes ingredients like lecture  Bionic acid and glue kono lactone  they're less well researched but they're  supposed to work as well as other  hydroxy acids but with less irritation  one of the problems with hydroxy acid  exfoliants is that alpha hydroxy acid  have been found to make your skin more  Sun sensitive so it will end up burning  faster this sensitivity isn't just when  the alpha hydroxy acid is on your face  so you don't need to only use them at  night but you do need to make sure that  you wear sunscreen every day when you  have alpha hydroxy acids in your routine  as well as for a week after you stop  using them  there are also some exfoliants that use  enzymes which can break down proteins  helping your skin exfoliate the most  common ones in skincare products are  bromelain from pineapple / pain from  papaya and act in attained from kiwi  fruit these exfoliants are usually used  like a mask you put them on for around  10 minutes before you wash them off you  can DIY an enzyme exfoliant by mashing  up the raw fruit and putting it on your  face canned fruit doesn't work as well  because when you can a fruit you have to  heat it up to a really high temperature  and this denatures or changes the shape  of the enzymes which means that they  don't work anymore I'm really lazy with  my enzyme peels so I usually just DIY  them if I've been eating kiwi fruit for  example I'll just save the skins and  then keep it in the fridge and rub it on  my face right before I go and have a  shower  [Music]  how much exfoliation your skin needs  depends on your skin type skin  conditions and the rest of your skincare  routine I've put more detailed  recommendations for different skin types  and conditions dry skin oily skin aging  skin acne prone skin sensitive skin and  so on in my free exfoliation guide but  here are some general tips when you  start using exfoliants I usually  recommend that you pick one gentle  exfoliant and start using it around once  a week and then if that goes well then  start increasing how frequently you use  it so start using it twice a week three  times a week and upwards then if you  still want more exfoliation you can add  a second exfoliant from a different  category so if you started with a  chemical exfoliant you would add a  physical exfoliant next or if you  started on a physical exfoliant you  would add a chemical exfoliant you  should be waiting a few weeks before you  add a new product to lower the chance of  your skin having a bad reaction it also  lets you see which products are actually  working which means it will be easier on  your wallet  and it'll stop your skincare routine  from jacking up way too much of your  time you should always start and build  up your routine slowly it's always much  better to under exfoliate than to over  exfoliate because it can take weeks for  over exfoliated skin to finally recover  when you exfoliate too much your skin  gets sensitive tight red and overly  shiny if you see any of these issues  start to crop up on your face then you  should stop exfoliating for at least a  few days and who your skin starts  recovering and then if you want to start  again again start really really slowly  going slow is especially important when  you're starting on new products and  especially if your skin is quite  sensitive  make sure you patch test new products  before you put them on your whole face  so if anything goes wrong it's much  easier to hide and ill hurt less and  that's a quick guide to exfoliation I've  put a link to my free exfoliation guide  in the caption below if you want all of  this info and much more and a guide that  you can print out and refer to when  you're working out your skin care  routine I also have more product  recommendations and short reviews in  there and I've linked some products in  the caption as well if you like this  video please like and subscribe to my  channel you can also find out more about  the science behind skincare and other  beauty products on my blog which is lab  math and calm if you want more regular  beauty tips you can also follow me on  Facebook and Instagram see you next time  for more beauty and science  [Music]

